#. settings-schema.json->show-cpu->tooltip
msgid ""
"Turbo boost, and the governor and energy preference where the power profile "
"is not the one writing them. What the processor is doing - frequency, "
"scaling driver, temperature - is under Sensors."
msgstr ""

#. settings-schema.json->show-devices->description
msgid "Show Devices"
msgstr ""

#. settings-schema.json->show-devices->tooltip
msgid "Batteries, chargers and anything else connected that carries a charge."
msgstr ""

#. settings-schema.json->show-sensors->description
msgid "Show Sensors"
msgstr ""

#. settings-schema.json->show-sensors->tooltip
msgid ""
"Temperatures, fans and power meters, under the name of the chip each came "
"off, and what the processor is running at."
msgstr ""

#. settings-schema.json->show-all-sensors->description
msgid "Include disk, network and board sensors"
msgstr ""

#. settings-schema.json->show-all-sensors->tooltip
msgid "When off, only CPU and GPU sensors are listed."
msgstr ""

#. settings-schema.json->monitor-brightness->description
msgid "Control external monitor brightness"
msgstr ""

#. settings-schema.json->monitor-brightness->tooltip
msgid ""
"For monitors on a cable, which have no kernel backlight. Needs ddcutil "
"installed and permission on /dev/i2c-*, and is only used on machines with no"
" built-in backlight of their own."
msgstr ""

#. settings-schema.json->control-section->description
msgid "Controls"
msgstr ""

#. settings-schema.json->enable-privileged-controls->description
msgid "Allow changing privileged power settings"
msgstr ""

#. settings-schema.json->enable-privileged-controls->tooltip
msgid ""
"CPU governor, energy preference, boost, ACPI platform profile and charge "
"limit are owned by the kernel and applied through pkexec, so an "
"administrator password is requested."
msgstr ""

#. settings-schema.json->scroll-action->description
msgid "Mouse wheel over the applet"
msgstr ""

#. settings-schema.json->scroll-action->tooltip
msgid ""
"The wheel has no screen in mind, so where the brightness of monitors on a "
"cable is what it moves, it moves all of them together. The sliders in the "
"menu are one per monitor."
msgstr ""

